1. `Hashing characters in c++` : `vector <char> d(256,0);` why 256 because characters will convert as per 
    ascii number and so we have to do 256 to unable almost all possible characters and then we can check 
    any character and increment that character's corresponding index. 
    
    
2. `is_sorted(d.begin(),d.end()` : by this we are checking whether are d vector is sorted in non descending 
    order or not,  if array is sorted in non descending order  then this will return true otherwise false . 
    
    
3.  `upper_bound` : by using upper_bound we can find a element which is exactly bigger than the element we 
    gave as a input. And we have to create a iterator data type to store its answer `auto k = upper_bound(d.begin(),d.end(),m); `
    then this will point on the exact position on the element which is strictly greater than m and then we
    can find index of that position using distance as `cout << distance(d.begin(),k);` this distance takes
    iterator but returns in integer . 
